 "cells": [
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Image Tensor Manipulation: How Machines \"See\"\n",
    "\n",
    "Welcome to the first notebook of **Term 02: Unstructured Data**. \n",
    "\n",
    "Before we dive into Neural Networks (CNNs), we must understand our raw material: **Tensors**. In this class, we will learn how to load images, manipulate their shapes, and prepare them for Deep Learning models using `torchvision` and `plotly`."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 1. Environment Setup\n",
    "\n",
    "First, we import the necessary libraries. We will use `torchvision.io` to read images directly as tensors."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import torch\n",
    "import torchvision\n",
    "import torchvision.transforms as T\n",
    "import plotly.express as px\n",
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "from PIL import Image\n",
    "import requests\n",
    "from io import BytesIO\n",
    "\n",
    "print(f\"Torch version: {torch.__version__}\")\n",
    "print(f\"Torchvision version: {torchvision.__version__}\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 2. Loading an Image\n",
    "\n",
    "Usually, you would load an image from your local `data/` folder. Since we don't commit data to GitHub, let's download a sample image from the internet to work with."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Download a sample image (e.g., a cute cat)\n",
    "url = \"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/Cat03.jpg/640px-Cat03.jpg\"\n",
    "response = requests.get(url)\n",
    "img_pil = Image.open(BytesIO(response.content))\n",
    "\n",
    "# Convert PIL to Tensor\n",
    "# Note: ToTensor() automatically scales pixels to [0, 1] and changes shape to (C, H, W)\n",
    "transform = T.ToTensor()\n",
    "img_tensor = transform(img_pil)\n",
    "\n",
    "print(f\"Tensor shape: {img_tensor.shape}\")\n",
    "print(f\"Dtype: {img_tensor.dtype}\")\n",
    "print(f\"Value range: [{img_tensor.min()}, {img_tensor.max()}]\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 3. Visualization with Plotly\n",
    "\n",
    "Plotly Express provides an interactive way to inspect our images. However, Plotly expects images in `(Height, Width, Channels)` format, while PyTorch uses `(Channels, Height, Width)`. We need to **permute** the dimensions."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Permute from (C, H, W) to (H, W, C)\n",
    "img_for_plot = img_tensor.permute(1, 2, 0)\n",
    "\n",
    "fig = px.imshow(img_for_plot, title=\"Interactive Cat Tensor\")\n",
    "fig.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 4. Grayscale vs. RGB\n",
    "\n",
    "What happens if we remove the color? A grayscale image has only 1 channel."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "gray_transform = T.Grayscale()\n",
    "gray_tensor = gray_transform(img_tensor)\n",
    "\n",
    "print(f\"RGB shape: {img_tensor.shape}\")\n",
    "print(f\"Grayscale shape: {gray_tensor.shape}\")\n",
    "\n",
    "# Plotting grayscale (we squeeze the channel dimension for plotly)\n",
    "px.imshow(gray_tensor.squeeze(), color_continuous_scale=\"gray\", title=\"Grayscale Representation\").show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 5. Basic Augmentations\n",
    "\n",
    "Deep Learning models need variety. We can use `torchvision.transforms` to create modified versions of our images."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "augmentations = T.Compose([\n",
    "    T.RandomHorizontalFlip(p=1.0),\n",
    "    T.RandomRotation(degrees=45),\n",
    "    T.CenterCrop(size=(300, 300))\n",
    "])\n",
    "\n",
    "augmented_tensor = augmentations(img_tensor)\n",
    "\n",
    "px.imshow(augmented_tensor.permute(1, 2, 0), title=\"Augmented Tensor\").show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "---\n",
    "## 🎓 Student Exercises\n",
    "\n",
    "### Exercise 1: Channel Slicing\n",
    "Create 3 separate plots showing only the **Red**, **Green**, and **Blue** channels of the original image.\n",
    "*Hint: `img_tensor[0, :, :]` is the Red channel.*\n",
    "\n",
    "### Exercise 2: The \"Mystery\" Tensor\n",
    "Generate a random tensor of shape `(3, 100, 100)` using `torch.rand`. Normalize it so its values are between 0.5 and 1.0, then visualize it.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Exercise 3: Normalization Check\n",
    "Take the `img_tensor` and calculate its **Mean** and **Standard Deviation** across all pixels. Then, apply `T.Normalize(mean=[...], std=[...])` using those values. What does the resulting image look like when plotted?"
   ]
  }\
 ]\n",
